<BaseLayout
  title="About"
  description="Purpose, nonprofit status, and working hypotheses of The Center for the Study of Anomalous Virtues, a Utah nonprofit (EIN 42-5016729)."
>
  <PageHeader
    kicker="About the Center"
    title="A hybrid human–AI think tank for moral inquiry."
    lede="CAV maps, analyzes, and synthesizes the philosophical and ethical underpinnings of moral values across human demographics — asking whether humanity shares a unified moral grammar or whether values are fundamentally relative to context."
  />

  <div class="mx-auto max-w-6xl px-5 py-12 sm:px-8">
    <article class="measure prose-cav text-[1.05rem]">
      <h2>Purpose</h2>
      <p>
        The Center for the Study of Anomalous Virtues is organized as a hybrid
        human–AI think tank at the intersection of computational humanities,
        comparative philosophy, and evolutionary ethics. We research anomalous
        virtues — moral qualities, strengths, or excellent traits that are unusual,
        unexpected, or seemingly contradictory in context — and educate the public,
        scholars, diplomats, mediators, and institutions in careful comparative
        understanding.
      </p>
      <p>
        Untranslatable culture-words remain central under Semantic Ethics. The
        research frame is wider: philosophy and ethics (contextual virtues, honor
        among thieves, flawed heroes) and literature and character design
        (sympathetic villains, reluctant saviors) also belong. The phrase
        <em>anomalous virtues</em> is not a single classical ethics term; it is a
        working designation drawn from those intersections.
      </p>

      <h2>Research program</h2>
      <p>
        Four pillars structure inquiry; Practice applies findings without
        overclaiming. Full methods appear on the
        <a href="/research">Research</a> page.
      </p>
      <ul>
        <li>
          <strong>Cultural &amp; Geographical Variations (Ethno-Ethics)</strong> —
          landscapes, scarcity, honor versus dignity, collectivism versus individualism.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Religious &amp; Theological Foundations (Sacred Axiology)</strong> —
          divine command, karmic and immanent traditions, secular humanist ethics.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Linguistic Relativity in Morals (Semantic Ethics)</strong> —
          untranslatable virtues (Ubuntu, Filotimo/Philotimo, Chesed, and others),
          grammatical framing of blame; precise bridges where generic language fails.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Temporal &amp; Historical Evolution (Chronological Ethics)</strong> —
          Axial Age convergence; warrior → Enlightenment → digital virtues.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Practice</strong> — applied engagement with training, diplomacy, and
          institutions, held to the limits of what research can support.
        </li>
      </ul>

      <h2>Working hypotheses <span class="font-serif text-base font-normal text-muted">(provisional)</span></h2>
      <p>
        These are labeled provisional. They orient inquiry; they are not findings.
      </p>
      <ul>
        <li>
          <strong>Moral Baseline.</strong> Evolutionary pressures may have produced a
          shared “moral grammar” — recurring concerns such as fairness, harm reduction,
          and in-group loyalty — that appears across many human populations.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Contextual Overlayer.</strong> Expression of that baseline is shaped
          by language, geography, historical trauma, theology, and institutions —
          producing the variation that looks, from the outside, like deep relativity.
        </li>
      </ul>
      <p>
        The research question is whether synthesis can distinguish baseline from
        overlayer without inventing a false universal or dissolving into pure
        relativism.
      </p>

      <h2>Nonprofit status</h2>
      <p>
        CAV is a Utah nonprofit corporation, The Center for the Study of Anomalous
        Virtues, organized exclusively for charitable, educational, and scientific
        purposes. It has no voting members; corporate powers rest with a board of
        directors.
      </p>
